#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:35
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:37
msgid "The hydraulic design of both the enclosed and open stacked rapid sand filter is complicated by the number of parallel flows, the manifolds in series, and the need to handle both backwash and filter modes. The design requires careful attention to weir hydraulics to achieve the target backwash flows when the plant is operating at less than full capacity. The siphon system has a tight design constraint to ensure that the siphon doesn't trigger prematurely."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:42
msgid "Filter Inlet Channel with Rectangular Weir Flow Distribution"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:44
msgid "In plants with flow rates large enough to use open stacked rapid sand filters the settled water is delivered to those filters through an open channel. The water exits the channel by flowing across a rectangular weir (see :numref:`figure_Filter_channel`). As is the case in a manifold pipe the water in the channel is decelerating and thus the piezometric head is increasing in the direction of flow. This increase in piezometric head is equivalent to the increase in the depth of water in the channel. This increase in water depth results in more water flowing across the final weir exiting the channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:53
msgid "Filter channel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:53
msgid "The filter inlet channel distributes flow to all of the filters. The water in the channel flows across sharp crested weirs into the filter inlet boxes. The velocity in the channel decreases in the direction of flow and thus the kinetic energy of the flow is converted into height. That added height results in greater flow into downstream filter inlet boxes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:55
msgid "The flow across the weirs into the filter inlet boxes is complicated by several factors. First, there must be a *vena contracta* as the flow changes direction to flow across the weir and thus the :math:`90^{\\circ}` *vena contracta* coefficient should enter the equations. Second, the weirs as they are fabricated are neither sharp nor broad and thus it isn't clear which equations are best suited. Sharp crested weirs are known to have a reduced depth of flow above the weir due to the acceleration of water approaching the weir and this effect is normally ignored and then thrown into the weir coefficient. Given that our weirs do not have a rounded upstream edge required by broad crested weirs we will use the sharp crested weir equation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:58
msgid "Side Exit Sharp Crested Weir"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:60
msgid " Q = \\Pi_{vc}\\frac{2}{3} \\sqrt{2g} w \\left(H_{channel}\\right)^\\frac{3}{2}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:66
msgid "where :math:`H_{channel}` is the height of the water in the channel above the top of the weir. (see equation 10.30 in Fundamentals of Fluid Mechanics, Fifth Edition by Munson, Young, and Okiishi)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:69
msgid "Inlet Channel Design for Equal Filter Flow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:71
msgid "We will simplify this manifold problem by assuming that the average water height in the channel above the weirs corresponds to the average flow across the weirs and that the upstream depth is decreased by 1/2 of the channel velocity head and the downstream depth is increased by 1/2 the channel velocity head."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:75
msgid "The ratio of flows from the first filter and the last filter in the channel is given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:77
msgid "\\Pi_{Q_{weir}} = \\frac{Q_{Filter_1}}{Q_{Filter_n}} = \\frac{\\Pi_{vc}\\frac{2}{3} \\sqrt{2g} w \\left(\\bar H_{channel} - \\frac{\\bar v_{M_1}^2}{4g}\\right)^\\frac{3}{2}}{\\Pi_{vc}\\frac{2}{3} \\sqrt{2g} w \\left(\\bar H_{channel} + \\frac{\\bar v_{M_1}^2}{4g}\\right)^\\frac{3}{2}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:83
msgid "where :math:`\\bar H_{channel}` is the average height of water in the channel relative to the top of the weir. Equation :eq:`Sharp_weir_flow_ratio_messy` simplifies to"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:85
msgid "\\Pi_{Q_{weir}} = \\frac{ \\left(\\bar H_{channel} - \\frac{\\bar v_{M_1}^2}{4g}\\right)^\\frac{3}{2}}{\\left(\\bar H_{channel} + \\frac{\\bar v_{M_1}^2}{4g}\\right)^\\frac{3}{2}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:90
msgid "The slower the velocity in the channel the more uniform the flow distribution will be between the filters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:92
msgid "Solve for the maximum velocity in the channel given the average depth of water above the weirs and the required flow distribution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:94
msgid " \\bar H_{channel}\\Pi_{Q_{weir}}^\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{\\bar v_{M_1}^2}{4g}\\Pi_{Q_{weir}}^\\frac{2}{3}= { \\bar H_{channel} - \\frac{\\bar v_{M_1}^2}{4g}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:99
msgid "Now we can solve for maximum manifold channel velocity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:101
msgid "\\bar v_{M_1} =  2\\sqrt{g\\bar H_{channel}\\frac{\\left(1-\\Pi_{Q_{weir}}^\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\Pi_{Q_{weir}}^\\frac{2}{3} + 1\\right)}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:107
msgid "The channel depth of water above the weir, :math:`\\bar H_{channel}`, and the flow uniformity target set the maximum velocity in the manifold channel (see :numref:`figure_Filter_channel_v_max`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:109
msgid "`Generate the following plot <https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1znzBGYHV1RXGqRz3Xm8Oyp7NQmAmkat6#scrollTo=MyYpzYxOU5px&line=5&uniqifier=1>`_"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:119
msgid "Filter channel velocities"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:119
msgid "The maximum velocity in the filter inlet channel decreases as the target flow ratio, :math:`\\Pi_{Q_{weir}}`, approaches 1. This graph was created assuming :math:`\\bar H_{channel}` of 5 cm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:124
msgid "Backwash Weir Slot Design"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:126
msgid "The goal of the backwash weir slot is to provide close to the design flow rate to a filter while it is in backwash mode. To accomplish this the wide gate weir is opened and the weir slot controls the flow of water into the inlet box. During backwash the water level in the inlet box is much lower and thus the backwash weir slot can extend deep into the box. The design constraint for this slot is that it must deliver the design flow when the water level in the inlet channel is at the design flow height and it must deliver at least 80% of the design flow  when there is no flow going to any of the other filters. The difference in water level between the two cases is :math:`H_{channel}` because this is the height of water flowing over the wide weir at the design flow rate. The height of the slot, :math:`H_{slot}`, is measured relative to the design flow water level in the inlet channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:128
msgid "This design will result in more water available for backwash than is absolutely needed and if it turns out that too much water is directed to this filter than the bottom of the slot can be elevated by adding a few stop logs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:130
msgid "The equation is based on the sharp crested weir (Equation :eq:`Sharp_weir_Q_of_channel_depth`). The head loss through the gate weir should be subtracted from both the top and bottom terms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:132
msgid "\\Pi_{Q_{BW}} = \\frac{Q_{BW_{min}}}{Q_{BW_{max}}} = \\frac{\\Pi_{vc}\\frac{2}{3} \\sqrt{2g} w \\left(H_{slot} - H_{channel} - HL_{Gate}\\right)^\\frac{3}{2}}{\\Pi_{vc}\\frac{2}{3} \\sqrt{2g} w \\left(H_{slot}- HL_{Gate}\\right)^\\frac{3}{2}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:137
msgid "Simplify and solve for :math:`H_{slot}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:139
msgid "   H_{slot} = \\frac{H_{channel}}{1-\\Pi_{Q_{BW}}^\\frac{2}{3}} + HL_{Gate}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:144
msgid "There are two possible constraints on the trunk size. Either the trunk size is dictated by backwash flow distribution requirements or the trunk size is dictated by the need to have uniform flow distribution between filter layers and hence to have exactly twice the flow rate through the inner inlets."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:146
msgid "There are 4 levels of flow distribution in StaRS filters. The direction of the design (top-down or bottom-up) is determined by the fact that when there is head loss in series that all of the head loss helps to achieve flow distribution. Thus the head loss through the orifices will be a required part of the design of the branches and that head loss will be an input for the trunk design. Thus we need to start at the bottom and work up."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:148
msgid "between orifices (branchPortQ_pi): made less important by the winged design that allows correcting flow in the winged space before the water enters the sand bed. This flow distribution does not benefit from the head loss through the sand. Suggest using a value of 0.8 for this constraint because of the balancing provided by the wings. The flow distribution constraint only provides a ratio of the port and branch velocities. The constraint for the maximum velocity allowable is either set by head loss or by the strength of the branch to span its length and not bend to much at the initiation of backwash. Either of those constraints can be converted into a maximum velocity for the inner branches and that will be used as an input to the design."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:150
msgid "The velocity constraint will determine the maximum length of a branch given its diameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:151
msgid "Use Equation :eq:`Manifold_max_v_no_hl_series` combined with the maximum branch velocity constraint to calculate the port velocity. Calculate the required branch diameter given the length (or vice versa)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:152
msgid "The orifice diameter will be selected based on constructability and not being too small to risk clogging (between 4 and 10 mm)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:153
msgid "Calculate the orifice spacing for the inner branches based on mass conservation and the maximum port velocity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:154
msgid "Calculate the maximum length of the branches given mass conservation and the maximum branch velocity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:156
msgid "between branches (trunkPortQ_pi): aided considerably by the head loss through the sand and is helped by the head loss though the orifices. Suggest using a value of 0.9 for this constraint. This constraint will be combined with a maximum permissible head loss during backwash to determine the required diameter of the trunk lines and will be combined with the equal trunk head loss constraint to obtain the diameter of the orifices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:158
msgid "Use Equation :eq:`Manifold_max_v_with_hl_series` to solve for the maximum trunk velocity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:159
msgid "Use the fact that the head loss is the same for outer and inner inlets to determine the :math:`K_{e_{outerOrifices}}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:161
msgid "between sand layers: easily obtained by simply requiring that inlet head losses be identical in the 4 inlets under conditions of the target flow and accounting for the fact that the inner inlets have double the flow of the outer inlets."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:162
msgid "between filters: will be handled by design of the weirs into the filter inlet boxes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:164
msgid "The clean bed sand head loss and including the head loss right at the point where the water enters the sand helps with the flow distribution between branches and between orifices. We are not currently including the benefit of the high velocity at the point where the water enters the sand."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:169
msgid "Design Steps"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:174
msgid "Inner Trunk"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:176
msgid "The diameter of the trunk lines is set by the filtration flow distribution between ports in the inner trunks and the maximum acceptable inlet head loss during backwash. The backwash head loss constraint is directly connected to the head loss during filtration because the velocity in the backwash trunk changes by a factor of the number of filter layers. The backwash inlet head loss sets the required depth of the open filter box and so is an important constraint for O-StaRS. Both the inner and outer inlet head loss during filtration is :math:`\\frac{1}{N_{layers}^2}` times the backwash inlet head loss because the head loss is proportional to the flow ratio squared and because we will set the inlet head losses to be identical during filtration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:178
msgid "The equation for flow distribution between branches with additional head loss in series is :eq:`Manifold_max_v_with_hl_series`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:180
msgid " \\frac{\\bar v_{T_{innerMax}}^2}{2g}=  \\left(h_{e_{B_{inner}}} + h_{e_{P_{inner}}} + h_{l_{sand}} \\right)\\Pi_{\\Psi_B}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:186
msgid "The head loss through the inner ports or orifices required to achieve reasonable flow distribution into the winged area of the inlet branches can be expressed in the minor loss equation form. The flow distribution constraint is given by Equation :eq:`Manifold_max_v_no_hl_series`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:188
msgid "h_{e_{P_{inner}}} = \\frac{\\bar v_{P_{inner}}^2}{2g} = \\frac{\\bar v_{B_{inner}}^2}{2g}\\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:193
msgid "where the port velocity :math:`\\bar v_{P_{inner}}` is the *contracted* velocity out of the orifice."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:195
msgid "The branch entrance loss is given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:197
msgid "h_{e_{B_{inner}}} = K_{e_B}\\frac{\\bar v_{B_{innerMax}}^2}{2g}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:202
msgid "The minor loss associated with entering the branch is given by Equation :eq:`he_branch`). The :math:`h_{l_{series}}` is the sum of the orifice head loss (see Equation :eq:`eq_he_port`) and the head loss through the sand. Making those substitutions into Equation :eq:`Trunk_to_branch_flow_distribution` we obtain"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:204
msgid " \\bar v_{T_{innerMax}}^2 =  \\Pi_{\\Psi_B}\\left[\\bar v_{B_{innerMax}}^2\\left(K_{e_B} + \\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}}\\right) + 2gh_{l_{sand}} \\right]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:209
msgid "This is a constraint on the maximum branch velocity assuming that the port velocity is set to achieve flow distribution to the wings within a branch (rather than setting the port velocity to achieve flow distribution between branches)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:211
msgid "This shows that the trunk velocity is limited by the branch velocity even without applying the head loss constraint. However, even if the branch velocity approaches zero, the trunk velocity can still be quite high because of the balancing effect of the sand head loss. This constraint ends up not being useful because flow division between branches is not a critical constraint."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:213
msgid "The head loss constraint is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:215
msgid "h_{e_{outerInlet_{Bw}}} = N_{layer}^2 h_{e_{innerInlet}} = N_{layer}^2 \\left(h_{e_{T_{inner}}} + h_{e_{B_{inner}}} + h_{e_{P_{inner}}} \\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:221
#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:492
msgid "The trunk entrance and elbow losses are given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:223
#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:494
msgid "h_{e_{T_{inner}}} = K_{e_T}\\frac{\\bar v_{T_{innerMax}}^2}{2g}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:228
#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:499
msgid "Substitute with minor loss relationships."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:230
msgid "2gh_{e_{outerInlet_{Bw}}} = N_{layer}^2 \\left(K_{e_T}\\bar v_{T_{innerMax}}^2 + K_{e_B}\\bar v_{B_{innerMax}}^2 + \\bar v_{B_{innerMax}}^2\\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}} \\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:235
#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:506
msgid "Solve for :math:`\\bar v_{T_{innerMax}}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:237
msgid "\\bar v_{T_{innerMax}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2gh_{e_{outerInlet_{Bw}}}}{K_{e_T} N_{layer}^2} -\\frac{\\bar v_{B_{innerMax}}^2}{K_{e_T}}\\left(K_{e_B} + \\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}} \\right)}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:242
msgid "The head loss constraint reveals that we can achieve the highest trunk velocity by setting the branch velocity to zero! This is because the branch head loss is not needed to achieve flow distribution between branches. Thus we will design the branches to have low velocities to increase the flow that can be achieved with a given size trunk. First define a dimensionless ratio of branch to trunk kinetic energy that will be used as a user specified constraint to allow the exploration of the tradeoff."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:244
msgid "\\bar v_{B_{innerMax}}^2 = \\Pi_{BT} \\bar v_{T_{innerMax}}^2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:249
msgid "Use Equation :eq:`Branch_Trunk_Pi` to eliminate :math:`\\bar v_{B_{innerMax}}` in Equation :eq:`he_T_inner_of_V`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:251
msgid "2gh_{e_{outerInlet_{Bw}}} = N_{layer}^2 \\left(K_{e_T}\\bar v_{T_{innerMax}}^2 + K_{e_B}\\Pi_{BT} \\bar v_{T_{innerMax}}^2 + \\Pi_{BT} \\bar v_{T_{innerMax}}^2\\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}} \\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:256
msgid "solve for :math:`\\bar v_{T_{innerMax}}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:258
msgid "\\bar v_{T_{innerMax}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{2gh_{e_{outerInlet_{Bw}}}}{N_{layer}^2 \\left[K_{e_T} + \\Pi_{BT}\\left(K_{e_B} + \\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}} \\right)\\right]}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:266
msgid "Inner branch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:268
msgid "Use Equation :eq:`Branch_Trunk_Pi` to solve for the maximum branch velocity given the results from Equation :eq:`V_trunk_of_HE_BW`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:270
msgid "Given the constraint of maximum branch velocity use the relationship between port velocity and branch velocity given by Equation :eq:`Manifold_max_v_no_hl_series` to solve for the port velocity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:272
msgid "\\bar v_{P_{innerMax}} = \\bar v_{B_{innerMax}}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:277
msgid "The orifice diameter will be constrained by the wing fabrication. Apply conservation of mass to obtain the port velocity to filter velocity ratio. Each port serves an area equal to the branch spacing times the port spacing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:279
msgid "\\frac{\\bar v_{P_{innerMax}}}{2 v_{Fi}} = \\frac{B_{branch} B_{orifice_{inner}}}{\\Pi_{vc}\\frac{\\pi}{4} D_{orifice}^2}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:284
msgid "where the factor of 2 is because the inner trunks serve two layers of sand. Combine equations :eq:`v_port_inner_branch` and :eq:`v_port_inner_to_v_Fi` and solve for the center to center spacing of the orifices."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:286
msgid "B_{orifice_{inner}} = \\frac{\\bar v_{B_{innerMax}}\\Pi_{vc}\\pi D_{orifice}^2}{8 v_{Fi}B_{branch}}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:291
msgid "We will assume that the user sets the target branch diameter as an input. The maximum length of a branch is set by mass conservation and the flow required to serve the filter area corresponding to the length of the branch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:293
msgid "2 v_{Fi} L_{branch_{max}} B_{branch} = \\bar v_{B_{innerMax}} A_{pipe}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:298
msgid "Solve for :math:`L_{branch_{max}}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:300
msgid " L_{branch_{max}} = \\frac{\\bar v_{B_{innerMax}} A_{pipe}}{2 v_{Fi}  B_{branch}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:305
msgid "Solve for the minimum pipe ID."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:307
msgid " D_{branch_{min}}= \\sqrt\\frac{8 v_{Fi}  B_{branch} L_{branch}}{\\pi \\bar v_{B_{innerMax}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:314
#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:515
msgid "At this stage in the design process we have set the flow rate through the filter, the trunk and branch diameters (except for the backwash branches), the length of the branches, and the orifice spacing on the inner inlets."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:319
#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:520
msgid "Outer branch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:321
msgid "The outer trunk branch orifices must be designed so that the head loss during filtration is identical between inner and outer inlets. This will result spacing between the outer branch orifices that is more than double that of the inner branch orifices. The derivation is similar to that used to obtain Equation :eq:`B_orifice_inner`. Equate the head loss in the inner and outer inlets during filtration. We will use the maximum velocity in the inner trunks as our reference velocity. Note that the results would be the same if we used the actual velocity in the inner trunks because the velocity will drop out of the equation in the end. First the head loss from the inlet box to the orifices in the inner inlets is given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:323
msgid "2gh_{e_{innerInlet}} = \\left(K_{e_T}\\bar v_{T_{inner}}^2 + K_{e_B}\\bar v_{B_{inner}}^2 + \\bar v_{B_{inner}}^2\\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}} \\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:328
msgid "Substitute Equation :eq:`Branch_Trunk_Pi` to eliminate :math:`\\bar v_{B_{inner}}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:330
msgid "2gh_{e_{innerInlet}} = \\left[K_{e_T} + \\Pi_{BT}\\left( K_{e_B}  + \\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}}\\right) \\right]\\bar v_{T_{inner}}^2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:335
msgid "The orifices for the outer inlets are not constrained by the flow distribution to the ports. Thus the factor :math:`\\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}}` does not apply. The unknown that we are solving for is port velocity which we will obtain from the ratio between port and branch kinetic energy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:337
msgid "\\bar v_{P_{outer}}^2 = \\Pi_{PB_{outer}} \\bar v_{B_{outer}}^2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:343
msgid "The head loss in the outer inlet is given by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:345
msgid "2gh_{e_{outerInlet}} = \\left(K_{e_T} + K_{e_B}\\Pi_{BT}  + \\Pi_{BT} \\Pi_{PB_{outer}} \\right)\\frac{1}{4}\\bar v_{T_{inner}}^2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:350
msgid "where the factor of 4 difference is because the velocity in the outer inlets is half the inner inlets because each inner inlet serves 2 filter layers. Now set the inner and outer head loss to be equal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:352
msgid "\\left(K_{e_T} + K_{e_B}\\Pi_{BT}  + \\Pi_{BT} \\Pi_{PB_{outer}} \\right)\\frac{1}{4}\\bar v_{T_{inner}}^2 = \\left(K_{e_T} + K_{e_B}\\Pi_{BT}  + \\Pi_{BT} \\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}} \\right)\\bar v_{T_{inner}}^2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:357
msgid "Simplify and solve for the unknown, :math:`\\Pi_{PB}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:359
msgid "\\Pi_{PB_{outer}}= \\left(3\\frac{K_{e_T}}{\\Pi_{BT}} + 3K_{e_B}  + 4\\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}} \\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:364
msgid "Apply conservation of mass to obtain the port velocity to filter velocity ratio. Each port serves an area equal to the branch spacing times the port spacing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:366
msgid "\\frac{\\bar v_{P_{outer}}}{v_{Fi}} = \\frac{B_{branch} B_{orifice_{outer}}}{\\Pi_{vc}\\frac{\\pi}{4} D_{orifice}^2}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:371
msgid "We need an equation for :math:`\\bar v_{P_{outer}}` as a function of :math:`\\bar v_{T_{inner}}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:373
msgid "\\frac{1}{4} \\bar v_{T_{innerMax}}^2 = \\bar v_{T_{outerMax}}^2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:378
msgid "\\bar v_{B_{outerMax}}^2 = \\Pi_{BT} \\bar v_{T_{outerMax}}^2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:383
msgid "\\bar v_{P_{outerMax}}^2 = \\Pi_{PB_{outer}} \\bar v_{B_{outerMax}}^2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:388
msgid "Combine the previous 3 equations to obtain"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:390
msgid "\\bar v_{P_{outerMax}} = \\frac{\\bar v_{T_{innerMax}}}{2} \\sqrt{\\Pi_{PB_{outer}}  \\Pi_{BT}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:395
msgid "The orifice spacing should be designed based on the maximum inner trunk velocity rather than the actual inner trunk velocity so that the branches have the same design for all filters. Otherwise the orifice spacing would be different for every design and that would only make fabrication needlessly confusing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:397
msgid "Substitute Equation :eq:`v_P_outer_of_v_T_inner` into Equation :eq:`v_port_outer_to_v_Fi` and solve for :math:`B_{orifice_{outer}}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:399
msgid "B_{orifice_{outer}} = \\frac{\\bar v_{T_{innerMax}} \\Pi_{vc} \\pi D_{orifice}^2 \\sqrt{\\Pi_{PB_{outer}}  \\Pi_{BT}} }{8 v_{Fi} B_{branch}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:404
msgid "The backwash branches have an additional constraint. Those branches have two additional challenges. First, during backwash the sand doesn't provide head loss to help equalize flow. Second, the velocity is :math:`N_{layer}` faster than during filtration. We will ensure that the flow is reasonably distributed by applying the flow distribution requirement without any additional head loss in series. We will use the orifice spacing that is used for the outer inlets."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:409
msgid "High Flow Design"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:411
msgid "The flow rate through the filter is severely limited if we keep the constraint that the head loss in the backwash inlet and in the inner inlets be the same. We can uncouple the head loss during the two states by having a removable orifice that can be added to the backwash inlet pipe during filtration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:416
msgid "Design inputs discussion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:418
msgid "We could start with a filter width (required by the control system) or a structural constraint given a nominal diameter of the branches. If we start with these two constraints, overall filter width and ND of the branches the branch velocity can be calculated from mass conservation. The complication is that the trunks create an inactive zone in the filter whose width will be a function of the diameter of the trunk and an iterative solution may be required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:420
msgid "A design goal might be to use identical branches for filters of different sizes. That would require setting the branch length as an input rather than the filter width. This might be reasonable and in any case the maximum branch length is a function of the branch ND."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:422
msgid "Design steps:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:424
msgid "find the maximum velocity in the outlet branches to get flow distribution through the slots using the filter clean bed head loss. assume branch length and branch ND (or an array of paired options)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:425
msgid "calculate branch velocity from mass conservation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:426
msgid "calculate max trunk velocity using Equation :eq:`V_trunk_of_he`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:427
msgid "size the trunk, then calculate number of filters, flow per filter, filter width, filter length"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:431
msgid "What are the failure modes as we increase the velocity in the trunk?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:433
msgid "port velocities may have to increase if the sand head loss isn't sufficient to ensure flow distribution between branches. Higher port velocities could erode the sand under the wing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:434
msgid "increased inlet head loss requires a deeper filter and deeper inlet and outlet control boxes. It would seem reasonable to limit this head loss to something less than 20 cm given that the dirty bed head loss for the filter is approximately 80 cm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:435
msgid "outlet branches have a maximum velocity to achieve flow distribution through the slots."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:440
msgid "Outlet branch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:441
msgid "The velocity in the outlet branches must be limited to prevent the change in piezometric head in the branch from causing significant differences in the velocity through the slots. The head loss through the filter bed helps keep this flow uniform. We could increase the head loss through the slots to make this flow more uniform, but that is a big failure mode because it is already too easy for these slots to clog over time and thus that problem would be made even worse. Instead we should be designing the outlet branches to have as much slot area as is structurally possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:443
msgid "Flow distribution through the slots is described by Equation :eq:`Manifold_max_v_with_hl_series`. We will neglect the head loss through the slots because if done well it will be small compared with the head loss through the sand. We can check this assumption later!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:445
msgid "The maximum outlet branch velocity is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:447
msgid " \\bar v_{B_{outletMax}}= \\sqrt{2gh_{l_{sand}}\\Pi_{\\Psi}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:452
msgid "This velocity is approximately 0.6 m/s and may be high enough so that it may not be a constraint. It isn't necessary that the flow distribution be extremely uniform given that as the sand bed head loss increases the flow distribution will improve. It will be included in the design code to ensure that we don't miss this constraint."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:457
msgid "Inlet branch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:458
msgid "Use mass conservation to determine the velocity in the branch given the branch length, ID, spacing and the filter velocity. The following equation could be corrected for the receptor length. The entire OD of the trunk should be counted as inactive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:460
msgid "v_{B} = \\frac{8 v_{Fi} B_{B} L_{B}}{\\pi D_{B}^2}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:465
msgid "The orifice diameter will be constrained by the wing fabrication. Apply conservation of mass to obtain the port velocity to velocity ratio. Each port serves an area equal to the branch spacing times the port spacing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:467
msgid "\\frac{\\bar v_{P}}{N_{sided} v_{Fi}} = \\frac{B_{B} B_{P}}{\\Pi_{vc}\\frac{\\pi}{4} D_{P}^2}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:472
msgid "where :math:`N_{sided}` is 2 for inner trunks that serve two layers of sand. Combine equations :eq:`v_port_inner_branch` and :eq:`v_port_inner_to_v_Fi` and solve for the center to center spacing of the ports."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:474
msgid "B_{P_{inner}} = \\frac{\\bar v_{B}\\Pi_{vc}\\pi D_{P}^2}{8 v_{Fi}B_{B}}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:482
msgid "Trunk Diameter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:484
msgid "The head loss for the inner inlets is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:486
msgid "h_{e_{innerInlet}} = \\left(h_{e_{T_{inner}}} + h_{e_{B_{inner}}} + h_{e_{P_{inner}}} \\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:501
msgid "2gh_{e_{innerInlet}} = \\left(K_{e_T}\\bar v_{T_{innerMax}}^2 + K_{e_B}\\bar v_{B_{inner}}^2 + \\bar v_{B_{inner}}^2\\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}} \\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:508
msgid "\\bar v_{T_{innerMax}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{K_{e_T}}\\left[2g  h_{e_{innerInlet}} -\\bar v_{B_{inner}}^2\\left(K_{e_B} + \\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}} \\right)\\right]}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:513
msgid "Use Equation :eq:`V_trunk_of_he2` to find the maximum trunk velocity. Use that constraint and the plant flow rate to find the trunk diameter, the number of filters, the filter flow rate, filter width, and filter length."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:522
msgid "The outer trunk branch orifices must be designed so that the head loss during filtration is identical between inner and outer inlets. This will result spacing between the outer branch orifices that is more than double that of the inner branch orifices. The derivation is similar to that used to obtain Equation :eq:`B_orifice_inner`. Equate the head loss in the inner and outer inlets during filtration. We will use the maximum velocity in the inner trunks as our reference velocity. Note that the results would be the same if we used the actual velocity in the inner trunks because the velocity will drop out of the equation in the end."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:524
msgid "The head loss from the inlet box to the orifices in the inner inlets is given by Equation :eq:`he_T_inner_of_V`. The head loss in the top inlet is similar. We will likely treat the backwash inlet as a separate design. The unknown we are solving for is the port velocity for the top inlet. That port velocity is not constrained by flow distribution and so we will enter it directly in the head loss equation knowing that all of the port kinetic energy is lost."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:526
msgid "2gh_{e_{topInlet}} = \\left(K_{e_T}\\bar v_{T_{top}}^2 + K_{e_B}\\bar v_{B_{top}}^2 + \\bar v_{P_{top}}^2 \\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:531
msgid "Assuming that we use the same size branches and trunks for the top and inner inlets, then the velocities in the top trunk and branch are 1/2 of the velocities in the inner trunk and branch."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:533
msgid "\\left(K_{e_T}\\bar v_{T_{inner}}^2 + K_{e_B}\\bar v_{B_{inner}}^2 + \\bar v_{B_{inner}}^2\\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}} \\right) = \\frac{1}{4} \\left(K_{e_T}\\bar v_{T_{inner}}^2 + K_{e_B}\\bar v_{B_{inner}}^2 \\right) + \\bar v_{P_{top}}^2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:538
msgid "Solve for the port velocity, :math:`v_{P_{top}}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:540
msgid "\\bar v_{P_{top}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4} \\left(K_{e_T}\\bar v_{T_{inner}}^2 + K_{e_B}\\bar v_{B_{inner}}^2\\right) +  \\frac{\\bar v_{B_{inner}}^2}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_P}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:546
msgid "The port spacing can be obtained from Equation :eq:`B_P_top`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:548
msgid "B_{P_{top}} = \\frac{\\Pi_{vc} \\pi D_{P}^2\\bar v_{P_{top}}}{4 v_{Fi}B_{B}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:556
msgid "Backwash_Inlet"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:557
msgid "The backwash inlet design is dominated by the flow distribution under backwash conditions when there is no head loss after the ports to promote flow distribution. Flow distribution can always be improved by increasing the port velocity and hence head loss and thus the maximum head loss is a second constraint."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:559
msgid "The backwash branch requires some flow distribution to ensure that the sand bed fluidizes along the entire length of the pipe. This raises the question of what happens when the sand bed begins fluidizing and part of the branch is in fluidized sand and part of the branch is buried in settled sand. Either the interface between the settled sand and the fluidized sand moves into the settled sand and the bed is slowly completely fluidized or the interface moves toward the fluidized sand and much of the sand bed never fluidizes. The sand bed will form the angle of repose and thus the toe of the solid sand bed will be narrow. This toe is likely eroded by the water. Given that water velocity leaving the wing is much higher than is needed to fluidize the sand (because the wing is narrower than the spacing of the branches) there is plenty of fluid energy to erode the toe of the sand and fluidize it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:561
msgid "Another possible mechanism is erosion of the sand under the wing based on the high horizontal velocity of the water in the wing as the water travels in the direction of the pipe axis toward the fluidized bed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:563
msgid "In either case, it appears that the wing design results in high velocity at the toe of and settled sand that can then rapidly erode and fluidize the entire bed. This suggests that the flow uniformity from the orifices into the winged space does not need to be great and so a factor of 0.8 is likely reasonable in Equation :eq:`Manifold_max_v_with_hl_series`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:565
msgid "The backwash inlet design is driven by the need for flow distribution at the port and branch levels and thus there are required relationships between port and branch and between branch and trunk velocities. In addition the total head loss will be a design constraint and thus we have 3 equations (2 flow distribution and 1 head loss) and 3 unknown velocities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:567
msgid "\\bar v_{P_{BW}}^2 = \\frac{\\bar v_{B_{BW}}^2}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_{P_{BW}}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:572
msgid "Flow distribution between the trunk and branches is more important than the flow distribution into the wings because a whole branch could remain unfluidized if it received significantly less water. Thus a higher flow distribution criteria of perhaps 0.9 could be applied to the trunk-branch system. The port head loss is available to help achieve this flow distribution. Thus Equation :eq:`Manifold_max_v_with_hl_series` applies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:574
msgid "\\bar v_{T_{BW}}= \\sqrt{2 g (h_{e_{B}} + h_{e_{P}})\\Pi_{\\Psi_{B_{BW}}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:579
msgid "where"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:581
msgid "h_{e_{B}} = K_{e_{B}}\\frac{\\bar v_{B_{BW}}^2}{2g}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:586
msgid "and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:588
msgid "h_{e_{P}} = \\frac{\\bar v_{P_{BW}}^2}{2g}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:593
msgid "Substitute to obtain a relationship between the three velocities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:595
msgid "\\bar v_{T_{BW}}^2= \\left( K_{e_{B}}\\bar v_{B_{BW}}^2 + \\bar v_{P_{BW}}^2\\right)\\Pi_{\\Psi_{B_{BW}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:600
msgid "Eliminate the port velocity by substituting Equation :eq:`v_P_to_v_B_BW` and solve for :math:`\\bar v_{B_{BW}}^2`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:602
msgid "\\bar v_{B_{BW}}^2 = \\bar v_{T_{BW}}^2 \\frac{1}{\\left( K_{e_{B}} + \\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_{P_{BW}}}} \\right)\\Pi_{\\Psi_{B_{BW}}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:608
msgid "The total head loss in the backwash inlet will be a design constraint."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:610
msgid "2gh_{e_{BW}} = \\left(K_{e_T}\\bar v_{T_{BW}}^2 + K_{e_B}\\bar v_{B_{BW}}^2 + \\bar v_{P_{BW}}^2 \\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:615
msgid "Substitute to obtain an equation for the maximum trunk velocity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:617
msgid "2gh_{e_{BW}} = \\left[K_{e_T}\\bar v_{T_{BW}}^2 + \\bar v_{B_{BW}}^2\\left(K_{e_B} + \\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_{P_{BW}}}}\\right) \\right]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:622
msgid "Substitute again to eliminate the branch velocity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:624
msgid "2gh_{e_{BW}} = \\bar v_{T_{BW}}^2\\left[K_{e_T} +  \\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_{B_{BW}}}} \\right]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:629
msgid "Solve for the maximum trunk velocity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:631
msgid "\\bar v_{T_{BWmax}} = \\sqrt\\frac{2gh_{e_{BW}}}{K_{e_T} +  \\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_{B_{BW}}}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:636
msgid "The backwash trunk may be the same diameter as the other trunk lines or it may be larger depending on the maximum velocities calculated from equations :eq:`V_trunk_of_he2` and :eq:`v_T_BW`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:638
msgid "The maximum branch velocity is now obtained by solving Equation :eq:`v_B_to_v_T_BW` for :math:`\\bar v_{T_{BW}}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:640
msgid "\\bar v_{T_{BW}} = \\bar v_{B_{BW}} \\sqrt{\\left( K_{e_{B}} + \\frac{1}{\\Pi_{\\Psi_{P_{BW}}}} \\right)\\Pi_{\\Psi_{B_{BW}}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:645
msgid "The branch minimum area is from Equation :eq:`branch_V`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:647
msgid "A_{B} = \\frac{N_{layer} v_{Fi} B_{B} L_{B}}{v_{B}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:652
msgid "The port velocity is obtained from Equation :eq:`v_P_to_v_B_BW` and the backwash port spacing is obtained by rewriting :eq:`v_P_to_v_Fi1` to include the relationship that the backwash velocity is the filtration velocity times the number of filter layers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:654
msgid "B_{P_{bw}} = \\frac{\\Pi_{vc} \\pi D_{P}^2\\bar v_{P_{BW}}}{4 v_{Fi} N_{layer} B_{B}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:662
msgid "Backwash Flow Control Orifice"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:664
msgid "The head loss through the backwash inlet system must be increased during filtration to match the head loss of the other inlets under conditions of ideal flow distribution between the filter layers. The orifice will likely be placed right on the inlet and thus this orifice will replace the entrance loss. The unknown is the inner diameter of the orifice. We know the expanded area (trunk area) and velocity and thus we can use the third form of the expansion head loss Equation :eq:`minor_loss_equation`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:666
msgid "   h_{e_{orifice}} = \\left( \\frac{A_{T}}{\\Pi_{vc}A_{orifice}} -1 \\right)^2 \\, \\frac{\\bar v_{T_{bw}}^2}{2g} \\, \\, = \\, \\, K_e \\frac{\\bar v_{T_{bw}}^2}{2g}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:672
msgid "Solve for the area of the orifice, :math:`A_{orifice}`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:674
msgid "   D_{orifice} =  \\frac{D_{T}}{\\left[\\Pi_{vc}\\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{2gh_{e_{orifice}}}}{\\bar v_{T_{bw}}} + 1\\right)\\right]^\\frac{1}{2}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:683
msgid "Backwash Siphon"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:685
msgid "The siphon manifold is designed to get reasonable flow distribution based on Equation :eq:`Manifold_max_v_no_hl_series`. The siphon diameter is based on dumping the water in the filter box in a reasonable amount of time, currently set to yield an average dump velocity in the filter box equal to the backwash velocity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:687
msgid "The siphon manifold is designed to have reasonable port flow distribution. The port flow distribution doesn't have to be very uniform because the sand-water interface has a very high density difference and thus is quite stable. Thus the velocity up out of the fluidized sand will not be harmed by a poor design of the manifold. A flow distribution, :math:`\\Pi_{Q}` of 0.8 will be very good. The required orifice area is obtained by solving :eq:`Manifold_max_v_no_hl_series` for the total port area."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:689
msgid " A_{P} = A_{M_1}\\sqrt{\\Pi_{\\Psi}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:694
msgid "where :math:`A_{P}` is the contracted port area."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:696
msgid "The ports will need to be large in diameter to achieve the required total port area. The port diameter must meet the following equation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:698
msgid "  floor\\left(\\frac{L_{manifold}}{D_P + S_P}\\right) \\Pi_{vc} \\frac{\\pi}{4}D_P^2 = A_P"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:703
msgid "where the first term is an estimate of the number of ports and the second term is the contracted port area. This equation can be solved by iteration over an array of drill bit sizes. A first estimate can be obtained by setting :math:`S_P = 0`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:705
msgid "  D_{P_{est}} = \\frac{4 A_P}{\\pi L_{manifold} \\Pi_{vc} }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:710
msgid "Use the quadratic equation to get a good estimate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:712
msgid "  L_{manifold} \\Pi_{vc} \\frac{\\pi}{4}D_P^2 - A_P D_P - A_P S_P = 0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:722
msgid "Backwash Initiation Forces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:724
msgid "At the beginning of backwash the sand is clogged and thus it requires more pressure to achieve the flow through the sand required to fluidize the sand. Instead, it is likely that one or more layers of sand begin to lift as a unit before falling apart and beginning to fluidize. During that transition the forces of the sand to lift the internal piping of the filter are quite large. We had structural failures in several of the early StaRS filters before we recognized the importance of a strong cable system to prevent the filter piping from lifting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:726
msgid "The maximum hydrostatic force acting on the bottom of the filter occurs when the inlet box is still full of water when the filter water depth is reduced by the siphon. The force in excess of the weight of the sand and water in the filter is equal to the height of water in the inlet box that is in excess of the backwash height of about 10 cm. This excess height is approximately equal to the terminal head loss through the filter during filtration, :math:`HL_{fi_{max}}`. The width of the filter bed that is contributing force to the receptor pipe is equal to 1/2 of the length of the branches. This force most likely acts uniformly on two layers of sand and associated piping at a time. The top two layers fluidize first when the water first stops going through the top inlet and thus all of the water is passing up through the top two layers. Thus the force is most likely shared by an inlet module and an outlet module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:728
msgid "There are two factors of 2 that show up in the equation. First, the total force is distributed between two layers. Second, half of the force from one side of the filter is carried by the trunk. Thus the force per unit length of the receptor pipe, :math:`\\omega`, in one module is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:730
msgid "\\omega = \\rho g h_{l_{sand_{max}}} \\frac{L_{branch}}{4}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:735
msgid "The total force acting upward that must be resisted by the receptor supports is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:737
msgid "F_{up} = \\rho g h_{l_{sand_{max}}} \\frac{Q_{Fi}}{v_{Fi} N_{layer}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:747
msgid "Receptor Pipe Support Spacing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:749
msgid "The optimal spacing of the supports allows the same deflection at the ends of the pipes (cantilever beam) and in the middle of the supports (beam simply supported at both ends). In both cases the load is uniformly distributed along the pipe (see `Beam Deflection Formulae <../_static/references/Beam_Deflection_Formulae.pdf>`_)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:751
msgid "\\delta_{max} = \\frac{\\omega L_{cantilever}^4}{8EI}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:756
msgid "The equivalent equation for a beam with simply supported ends is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:758
msgid "\\delta_{max} = \\frac{5\\omega L_{both}^4}{384EI}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:763
msgid "We'd like the deflection to be the same in the cantilever ends of the receptor pipe as in the middle sections of the pipe where it is simply supported. Set the deflections to be the same."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:765
msgid "\\frac{5\\omega L_{both}^4}{384EI} = \\frac{\\omega L_{cantilever}^4}{8EI}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:770
msgid "Solve for the ratio of the length of the respective beams"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:772
msgid "\\frac{ L_{cantilever}}{L_{both}} = \\left(\\frac{5 }{48}\\right)^\\frac{1}{4} = 0.57"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:777
msgid "This result is a bit surprising until we recognize that the cantilever beam is not allowed to rotate at the point where it is connected while the simple beam was allowed to rotate. The simple conclusion from all of this is that the cantilever sections can be 1/2 the length of the simply supported sections of the receptor pipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:779
msgid "We previously supported the receptor pipe at the ends. That wasn't optimal. With the new StaRS design it is possible to make larger filters and it isn't get clear what the optimal ratio of width to length of the filters will be. In any case, the length of the receptor pipe is likely to be significantly longer than in our previous designs for the highest flow rates. It will be necessary to ensure that the deflection isn't too large."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:781
msgid "The deflection of the receptor pipe is given by Equation :eq:`supported_at_both_ends`. The area moment of inertia, :math:`I` is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:783
msgid "I = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\left(OD^4 - ID^4\\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:788
msgid "The standard diameter ratio for PVC pipes is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:790
msgid "\\Pi_{SDR} = \\frac{OD}{t}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:795
msgid "Substituting Equation :eq:`SDR` into Equation :eq:`pipe_Ix_of_OD_ID`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:797
msgid "I = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\left[OD^4 - \\left(\\Pi_{SDR}t - t\\right)^4\\right]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:802
msgid "simplifying"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:804
msgid "I = \\frac{\\pi}{4}OD^4\\left[1 - \\left(1-\\frac{1}{\\Pi_{SDR}}\\right)^4\\right]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:809
msgid "If we set a maximum deflection, then we can solve Equation :eq:`supported_at_both_ends` for the maximum length between supports."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:811
msgid "L_{both} = \\left(\\frac{384EI\\delta_{max}}{5\\omega}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{4}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:816
msgid "Agua Para el Pueblo successfully used 2\" PVC pipe with SDR 17 for the receptor at Gracias where the support spacing was 1 meter, the filter length was 1.34 meter, filter width was 1.58 meter, and the branch length of 0.63 meter. The maximum receptor deflection was estimated to be about 2 mm under these conditions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:819
msgid "Branch Deflection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:821
msgid "Similar analysis could be performed on the branches to ensure that they don't deflect too much at the beginning of backwash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:826
msgid "Receptor Support Strength"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:828
msgid "The supports for the receptors could have a cable or a threaded rod coming up on both sides or only on one side. Single sided support would be preferred to reduce the extra width of the filter required to accommodate the double sided support. The single sided support would have the cable coming up on one side. When the sand lifts on the dead pipe the support will tend to rotate around the cable and put the support in compression on the other side of the cable. The question is then the required dimensions of the compression area given the strength of the PVC."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:830
msgid "The force acting on one receptor support is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:832
msgid "F_{up} = \\rho g h_{l_{sand_{max}}}\\frac{W_{filter}}{4} B_{support}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:837
msgid "This force must be resisted by the PVC support. Given that the lift force :math:`F_{up}` is on the order of 2000 N and the PVC compressive strength is approximately 55 MPa this requires less than a square centimeter of PVC. This suggests that a single sided support for the receptors with only one cable will be adequate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:840
msgid "Slotted Pipe Upgrade"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:842
msgid "Agua Para el Pueblo recommends an annual maintenance cleaning of the filter. The underlying issue is that the slots in the slotted pipes that extract the filtered water from the sand bed slowly clog over time. The filters then need to be backwashed more frequently. The sand is dumped from the filter while the filter is operating in backwash mode using a special sand dump pipe. The inner pipe modules are removed from the filter and then slotted pipes are washed with a pressure washer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:844
msgid "The slotted pipes can clog with calcium carbonate deposition or by having sand forced into the slots. Byron Zuniga at Agua Para el Pueblo reports that there is a significant amount of sand grains embedded in the slots that is best removed using a pressure water. The sand is likely forced into the slots at the initiation of backwash when the sand bed is lifting and causing the slotted pipes to bend upward. The slots above the midline of the branch would expand as the branch bends upward and thus a larger diameter branch that bends less could reduce the amount of sand that is embedded in the slots. One option is to increase the branch diameter form 1\" to 1.5\" to create a more rigid system. It would also help to reduce the length of the slots to make the PVC between the slots more rigid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:846
msgid "A more robust strategy is to devise a method to completely eliminate slotted PVC pipes from StaRS filters. One approach would be to use stainless steel slotted pipes to dramatically reduce the deformation of the pipe and thus prevent sand grains from being forced into the slots. Stainless steel pipes will increase the cost and there is some evidence that even stainless steel slotted underdrain systems clog over time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:848
msgid "The most attractive option is to develop a method to extract water from the sand bed without relying on slots. The AguaClara team has explored gravity exclusion zones, GEZ, as an alternative to slots. GEZ are designed to keep the fluid velocity below the sand fluidization velocity so that gravity prevents the water from carrying sand into the effluent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:850
msgid "At the end of backwash the sand forms horizontal surfaces in regions where sand can't fill in from above. The horizontal sand/water interface will remain stable as long as the vertical velocity of the water is lower than the fluidization velocity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:854
msgid "Sand Dump"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:856
msgid "The frequency of cleaning makes is imperative that the process of emptying the sand from the filter, removing the modules, cleaning the modules, replacing the modules, and replacing the sand be as easy as possible. One of the systems that is absolutely essential in a StaRS filter is the hydraulic sand dump that empties the sand from the filter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:858
msgid "The sand dump pipe must be designed so that if the flow of the sand slurry is stopped, that the sand doesn't collect at one location in the pipe and cause a clog. A horizontal section of pipe followed by a vertical upward section of pipe would cause a failure because all of the sand in the vertical section of pipe would settle into the elbow. Given the goal of completely emptying the sand from the filter it is necessary to elevate the exit of the sand slurry so that it isn't necessary to lower the floor level in the pipe gallery."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:860
msgid "The first version of sand extraction pipes for StaRS filters avoided this problem by having the vertical section of the pipe inside the filter. Thus any sand inside the vertical section of the pipe simply dropped back into the filter box when the flow through the sand dump was stopped. Placing the vertical section of the sand drain inside the filter box was a major challenge because it had to fit between the inlet and outlet piping that is in the sand bed. The section of the sand drain pipe inside the filter box also had to be removed in order to remove the inlet and outlet piping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:862
msgid "Slurry transport pipes used in mining operations avoid the sand clogging problem by using shallow sloped pipes where the slope of the pipe is less than the angle of repose of sand in water. The recommended slope is less than :math:`14^\\circ` and the fluid velocity to ensure that the sand doesn't settle is between 3 and 6 m/s. (see `Hydrotransport Pipelines: Basic Design Principles <https://www.oilsandsmagazine.com/technical/mining/hydrotransport/pipeline-design>`_)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:864
msgid "The discharge elevation of the sand dump must be low enough to achieve at least the required minimum velocity in the pipe from the time when the filter box is full of sand until it is empty. Given that the water elevation in the filter box is set to be 10 cm above the top of the siphon pipe, that level will remain constant as the sand is removed. The hydrostatic pressure at the bottom of the filter will decrease as the fluidized sand is replaced with water. Thus the lowest hydrostatic pressure will occur at then end of the sand dump process when the filter box is full of water."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:866
msgid "The goal will be to drain the sand as quickly as possible to limit the amount of water wasted. The sand bed must be fluidized during the operation and thus the upflow velocity in the sand bed will need to be about half of the backwash velocity. This flow rate and the target pipe velocity required to keep the sand moving (3 m/s) set the MAXIMUM pipe diameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:868
msgid "The available piezometric head to drive the sand slurry through the sand dump pipe is equal to the depth of water in the filter during backwash. The maximum elevation of the sand dump discharge must take into account the density of the sand slurry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:870
msgid "The maximum density of the slurry in the sand dump pipe would be the case where the sand is just beginning to fluidize with a porosity equal to the normal settled sand porosity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:872
msgid "\\rho_{sandbed} = \\frac{M_{water} + M_{sand}}{{\\forall_{sandbed}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:877
msgid "The mass of sand is given by its density and volume."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:879
msgid "\\rho_{sandbed} = \\frac{\\forall_{water} \\rho_{water} + \\forall_{sand}\\rho_{sand}}{{\\forall_{sandbed}}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:884
msgid "The water volume fraction is the porosity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:886
msgid "\\rho_{sandbed} = \\phi \\rho_{water} + (1-\\phi)\\rho_{sand}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:891
msgid "which has a value of about 2000 :math:`\\frac{kg}{m^3}` for silica sand. The total energy at the bottom of the sand dump pipe must equal the potential energy provided by the water column in the filter when the sand has all been removed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:894
msgid "\\rho_{water}g H_{water} = \\rho_{sandbed}g \\left(H_{discharge} + K\\frac{\\bar v^2}{2g}\\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:899
msgid "We will use a relatively short straight section of pipe as the sand drain and thus entrance and exit minor losses will dominate. The entrance will be a reentrant pipe and thus the total minor loss will be 2."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:901
msgid "Substitute Equation :eq:`density_suspension` for :math:`\\rho_{sandbed}` and solve Equation :eq:`sand_dump_energy_final` for the maximum discharge height."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:903
msgid "H_{discharge_{max}} = \\frac{\\rho_{water}H_{water}}{\\phi \\rho_{water} + (1-\\phi)\\rho_{sand}}  - K\\frac{\\bar v^2}{2g}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:908
msgid "There isn't sufficient head available in backwash mode to achieve the 3 m/s. We know that the sand drain system that has an outlet slightly below the filter sand level works fine. Presumably the velocity in the pipe is much less than the 3 m/s that is suggested as the minimum by the mining industry. It is also expected that the vertical pipe in the filter box would work with much lower velocities because there is no horizontal surface for the sand to settle on."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:910
msgid "If it is possible to force the sand out during filtration mode, then the available head is much larger. We will test the ability to unload the sand while in filtration mode at a plant in Honduras."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:912
msgid "The backwash trunk prevents sand from moving between sides of the filter and thus there should be a sand drain on each side of the trunks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:914
msgid "Instead of designing for the end of the sand removal process, design for the beginning when the hydrostatic pressure at the bottom of the filter is highest because of the column of fluidized sand. Under that assumption the left side of Equation :eq:`sand_dump_energy_final` changes to be the height of the sand column plus water."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:916
msgid "\\rho_{sandbed}g H_{sandbed} + \\rho_{water}g H_{water} = \\rho_{sandbed}g \\left(H_{discharge} + K\\frac{\\bar v^2}{2g}\\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:921
msgid "Solve for the maximum discharge height assuming we empty the sand in backwash mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:923
msgid " H_{discharge} = H_{sandbed} + \\frac{\\rho_{water}}{\\rho_{sandbed}} H_{water} - K\\frac{\\bar v^2}{2g}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Filtration/Filtration_Design.rst:928
msgid "The maximum sand dump height can either be achieved by dumping the sand in filtration mode or using backwash mode. In backwash mode the initial head will be sufficient to drive the sand through the sand dump, but as the sand empties the hydrostatic pressure at the bottom of the filter will decrease. The pressure could be increased by increasing the height of the siphon outlet."
msgstr ""
